00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.720
Hello, and welcome back to
another episode with ItsDrDan.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.870
And today we're going to be
learning about chemical bonding.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:09.639
So what is chemical bonding?

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:10.770
What is it all about?

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.680
So this is an introductory video,
just going over the different

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.839
types of chemical bonds and how
exactly, how do we come across them?

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.670
How do we label them?

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:19.460
What do they look like?

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.530
So for this one, uh, what exactly a
bond is, is when two or more atoms

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:29.280
combine where they form a bond,
meaning that they have some type of

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.580
force of attraction between them.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.449
Now, what is all the
driving force between these?

00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:36.650
Why do they want to bond so badly?

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.750
Well, one of the things that you've
been learning about in your chemistry

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:45.495
course is that Most atoms, they do
not have a complete outer shell,

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:48.015
meaning that they aren't quite happy.

00:00:48.075 --> 00:00:51.185
They want to have all
their electrons filled.

00:00:51.464 --> 00:00:52.705
They don't want to react.

00:00:52.714 --> 00:00:54.805
They want to be like a noble guest.

00:00:55.035 --> 00:00:59.184
The goal is to be dignified,
just like a noble when they look

00:00:59.184 --> 00:01:00.455
at these different elements.

00:01:00.775 --> 00:01:02.725
So how can you do that?

00:01:03.145 --> 00:01:05.895
Well, elements, they, what they're
going to do, they're going to combine.

00:01:06.274 --> 00:01:10.725
Meaning that when this happens,
we will fill our valence level.

00:01:10.764 --> 00:01:15.995
Remember, valence is your highest
quantum number, your outermost layer.

00:01:16.025 --> 00:01:18.204
Think of an onion, it's
the outside of the onion.

00:01:18.905 --> 00:01:24.034
So, now the goal is, is that you want
to have 8 electrons for these elements.

00:01:24.034 --> 00:01:26.154
So, many of them want to have 8.

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:29.814
There are exceptions, which we'll
talk about in other videos, but

00:01:29.814 --> 00:01:32.035
the goal is 8 for most of them.

00:01:32.234 --> 00:01:33.924
Now, how can they do it though?

00:01:34.214 --> 00:01:35.284
How can they get to 8?

00:01:35.775 --> 00:01:37.295
Well, they can do all kinds of things.

00:01:37.295 --> 00:01:39.725
How can you try to get, let's
say, if you want to think about

00:01:39.725 --> 00:01:40.755
money, how can you get there?

00:01:40.755 --> 00:01:43.935
Well, you could work for it, you
could borrow it, you could take

00:01:43.935 --> 00:01:46.685
it, you could lose it, you could
do all kinds of things with it.

00:01:47.125 --> 00:01:48.704
It's kind of the same idea with electrons.

00:01:49.114 --> 00:01:53.484
So what can they do is they could give
their electrons away, meaning that that

00:01:53.484 --> 00:01:55.465
will get them to an octet, possibly.

00:01:56.035 --> 00:02:01.325
They could take electrons from other
Atoms and in the generally it's

00:02:01.325 --> 00:02:03.165
like a bar like a bartering system.

00:02:03.165 --> 00:02:04.315
They'll be happy as well.

00:02:04.315 --> 00:02:09.175
So they don't mind losing them to write
give and take or we can share right now.

00:02:09.225 --> 00:02:10.574
Each of these have their own benefits.

00:02:10.585 --> 00:02:14.475
Different different types of atoms
will do all these different reactions.

00:02:15.015 --> 00:02:20.055
So when we see this type of bonding
that's occurring, well, we represent

00:02:20.055 --> 00:02:24.055
this either as compounds or as molecules.

00:02:24.065 --> 00:02:26.945
So you're going to hear your
different professors and teachers

00:02:27.325 --> 00:02:30.244
using these words a lot, and it's
going to get a little confusing.

00:02:30.584 --> 00:02:34.875
Keep in mind they generally
mean a very similar definition,

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:36.305
but they do have a difference.

00:02:37.085 --> 00:02:43.765
So compounds are whenever you have two
or more different types of atoms, meaning

00:02:43.765 --> 00:02:49.224
like if you have a metal and a non-metal
or a metalloid and a non-metal maybe

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.904
are generally metals and non-metals.

00:02:51.375 --> 00:02:54.734
So they're two different complete
different types of a of atoms.

00:02:55.125 --> 00:03:00.215
Now, what that means is all
compounds are molecules here.

00:03:00.615 --> 00:03:05.205
So molecules, if you notice, are
two or more atoms that are bonded.

00:03:05.245 --> 00:03:07.845
It doesn't matter what they
are, as long as they're bonded.

00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:12.395
So you could have a metal and a
metal, or a nonmetal and a nonmetal.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.969
You can have a metal and a non
metal, as long as they are bonded.

00:03:17.279 --> 00:03:20.469
So all compounds are molecules.

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:23.149
So you'll hear that word
kind of interchanged a lot.

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:28.659
Usually, we use compounds to
describe ionic species more often.

00:03:28.779 --> 00:03:32.429
Now, we'll talk about this throughout
here, of course, in all these

00:03:32.429 --> 00:03:34.230
videos, for you to understand that.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.270
So what does that mean in the end
is not all molecules are compounds.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.300
Remember, they have to be, in
order to be a compound, they

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.260
need to be two different types.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.210
That's the most important
thing for this sentence.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:52.830
Compounds, not all of them are
compounds, so compounds are

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.580
generally metals and nonmetals.

00:03:55.105 --> 00:03:58.095
All right, let's take a further
look at these different,

00:04:07.235 --> 00:04:11.585
so there are generally three types
of bonds that just as a general

00:04:11.615 --> 00:04:16.425
classification for general chemistry,
the first, the three of them are

00:04:16.465 --> 00:04:19.884
ionic, covalent, and metallic bonds.

00:04:20.165 --> 00:04:23.255
We're going to talk briefly about
each one of them so you get a

00:04:23.285 --> 00:04:25.485
basic idea of what they look like.

00:04:26.045 --> 00:04:31.245
So ionic is when one atom donates
an electron and the other one

00:04:31.325 --> 00:04:33.905
accepts, so it's that give and take.

00:04:33.905 --> 00:04:33.979
Okay.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:40.090
Now, when this happens, it's making an
electrostatic attraction, meaning it's

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:45.340
that positive and negative charge are
being attracted to each other, okay?

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.290
So positive and negative, right?

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.760
So this is where your predictable ions
are going to be very, very important.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.850
This is when metals act
as what we call cations.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.010
Remember, cats are positive.

00:04:58.955 --> 00:05:04.325
Non-Metals Act as anions or as I
kind of sometimes say as onions.

00:05:05.094 --> 00:05:08.705
Um, so anion and cat ions.

00:05:09.405 --> 00:05:13.395
Now these can exist in
different subcategories.

00:05:13.455 --> 00:05:16.185
They can be type one or type two.

00:05:16.724 --> 00:05:20.145
Now, type one means that
the cat ion is predictable.

00:05:20.385 --> 00:05:23.985
It's something that always has
the same charge no matter what.

00:05:24.375 --> 00:05:26.414
So it's usually one of the
elements found in your first

00:05:26.414 --> 00:05:28.094
two columns of your POC table.

00:05:28.545 --> 00:05:32.635
or maybe ammonium as a polyatomic,
something that's always the same.

00:05:33.445 --> 00:05:37.274
Type two is when you have a variable
charge, meaning that there is a

00:05:37.285 --> 00:05:39.655
transition metal that's bonding.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.150
Now, what do these guys look like?

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:48.650
Well, ionic compounds are always,
they exist as crystalline structures,

00:05:48.969 --> 00:05:52.229
meaning that if you've ever looked
at, let's say you have table salt at

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:56.189
home, so you salt different food dishes
all the time, it's a crystal, right?

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:58.189
It looks like a little rock that
comes out of your container.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:01.689
If you look at it under a really
powerful microscope, you actually

00:06:01.689 --> 00:06:03.259
will see the crystal structure.

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:10.235
If you ever go to, geode exhibit, You'll
also see that all the different rocks

00:06:10.245 --> 00:06:12.445
exist in crystalline structures as well.

00:06:12.984 --> 00:06:16.474
This is because they are ionic
compounds that exist as a crystalline

00:06:16.715 --> 00:06:21.724
lattice, meaning that they exist
in this repeating framework.

00:06:22.065 --> 00:06:27.304
So you have Na plus as yellow orbs here,
and chlorine minus as green, and you

00:06:27.305 --> 00:06:29.955
can see it's this alternating pattern.

00:06:30.395 --> 00:06:36.045
Now, the thing about this though is,
we, it's very hard to count the number

00:06:36.045 --> 00:06:40.545
of atoms and ions when you want to
illustrate these, so generally we like

00:06:40.555 --> 00:06:43.895
to simplify these to the simplest ratio.

00:06:44.335 --> 00:06:48.944
What we refer to this as is a
formula unit, okay, so that's

00:06:48.945 --> 00:06:50.305
the important word here.

00:06:50.844 --> 00:06:54.645
So now the good news is, this
formula unit is all based on your

00:06:54.645 --> 00:06:56.175
predictable, all based on your charges.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.860
So, for example, if I have, let's
say, Na and Cl here, remember

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:06.409
from before, the goal was 8, so
how do we get to 8 electrons?

00:07:06.855 --> 00:07:10.355
Well, what can happen is, remember,
it's all about giving and taking.

00:07:10.734 --> 00:07:16.534
So sodium has a, wants to give
away its one electron here.

00:07:17.125 --> 00:07:19.265
So what will it become if it does that?

00:07:19.575 --> 00:07:22.494
Well, then we'll have Na plus, right?

00:07:22.494 --> 00:07:26.334
And if you remember from your predictable
charges in the periodic table, that's

00:07:26.334 --> 00:07:28.875
exactly where that charge comes from.

00:07:29.395 --> 00:07:34.175
And then chlorine on the other
side will now have a complete

00:07:34.784 --> 00:07:37.534
Um, eight electrons as a result.

00:07:37.544 --> 00:07:40.494
So we can now fill in that
extra electron from sodium.

00:07:41.125 --> 00:07:46.155
And then we would write that in
brackets with a negative charge to

00:07:46.155 --> 00:07:51.655
say, like, hey, this guy does have
its complete negative, uh, charge.

00:07:51.925 --> 00:07:53.955
Sometimes this can also be
written where the negative is

00:07:53.965 --> 00:07:55.515
inside the brackets as well.

00:07:55.525 --> 00:07:57.194
It generally depends on this notation.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:02.219
But this is a general notation where
we use brackets to show that ion.

00:08:02.219 --> 00:08:03.010
So keep that in mind.

00:08:03.539 --> 00:08:06.120
I will put that this
is a general notation.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:14.219
Um, so we're all about trying to give and
take these different types of electrons as

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:16.099
we are looking at these different species.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:22.539
Now, overall, when we write the final
name for this, we would just write NaCl.

00:08:23.195 --> 00:08:31.385
And if you'd like to, you could label this
as an ionic type one compound, and it's

00:08:31.385 --> 00:08:34.565
also considered a binary ionic compound.

00:08:34.655 --> 00:08:36.095
Binary, meaning that there's two.

00:08:36.335 --> 00:08:37.655
So where do I get two from?

00:08:37.685 --> 00:08:39.245
There's an NA and a cl.

00:08:39.565 --> 00:08:39.924
It's two.

00:08:40.655 --> 00:08:43.295
So type one is that they're
predictable, being that they have

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.985
A plus as a NA has a plus charge,
and it always has a plus charge.

00:08:48.055 --> 00:08:49.765
So that's the general idea for Ionic.

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:51.265
Let's look at an example.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:55.310
I have an ionic compound here,
which is a metal plus a non metal.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:59.370
So we have calcium, which is our
metal, and fluorine, our non metal.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:04.750
So calcium, which is going to be our
cat ion here, is going to be likely to

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:07.310
give away its electrons to get eight.

00:09:07.855 --> 00:09:13.105
So when it gives away its electrons,
it will give one away to each fluorine,

00:09:13.655 --> 00:09:21.054
and as a result, we will have calcium 2
Okay, now what about all the fluorines?

00:09:21.065 --> 00:09:24.774
So what we're gonna do is we're gonna
put the fluorines on each side here.

00:09:25.459 --> 00:09:29.189
And we're now going to draw in all
the different electrons, so let's

00:09:29.189 --> 00:09:31.180
draw the ones that calcium gave first.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:34.880
And then we'll put the other
eight dots around this.

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:41.059
And now we can see that we have
a complete octet around fluorine.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:43.930
which makes it very, very happy.

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:48.400
And then we also have a complete
octet around calcium as well.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:53.060
Keep in mind that calcium does
have a complete octet because it

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:57.850
loses its outer shell electrons,
just as a kind of quick reminder.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:06.940
So it loses its 4s2 electrons and it
will have a final configuration Um, where

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:12.770
essentially it will be equivalent to
argon or its electron configuration for

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.119
those of you who are learning about that.

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:20.920
So its final valence level will
now be 3s2 3p6 and you can write

00:10:21.029 --> 00:10:23.810
that it has a neon over here.

00:10:24.239 --> 00:10:25.449
So, which is argon.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:26.030
Okay.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:28.750
So it also has those
eight valence electrons.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:31.450
Let's take a look at covalent.

00:10:32.069 --> 00:10:34.060
bonding to learn about the next time.

00:10:34.099 --> 00:10:38.360
So covalent bonding is all
about sharing of electrons.

00:10:38.744 --> 00:10:43.515
Meaning we try to make this as
equal of a partnership as possible.

00:10:44.075 --> 00:10:47.385
Now keep in mind, sometimes this
is not always the most equal.

00:10:47.465 --> 00:10:51.275
You generally can also have a little
bit, maybe it lean one way or the other.

00:10:51.655 --> 00:10:53.824
But let's just for now, keep it simple.

00:10:53.915 --> 00:10:54.674
It's about equal.

00:10:55.145 --> 00:10:59.435
So the way that this works instead is that
each one of these will kind of come in.

00:10:59.435 --> 00:11:01.415
So I'll bring a hydrogen on each side.

00:11:01.895 --> 00:11:04.474
And we literally share our electrons.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:11.830
So, as a result, hydrogen has a complete
external layer, and so does oxygen.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:16.630
They equally share all those
electrons that are around it.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:24.280
So we kind of can notice oxygen has
1,2,3,4,5, 6, 7, 8, and hydrogen

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.770
is one of those exceptions where
it can only actually have only

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.000
two, so everything is happy.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:36.310
So generally the way that we will kind
of illustrate this as a happy bond,

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:41.860
um, for covalent is we'll draw a line
to illustrate that we have a bond.

00:11:42.325 --> 00:11:45.215
So if we move all these different
species, we'll get rid of these

00:11:45.215 --> 00:11:51.454
electrons here, and we will write a
line to represent a chemical bond.

00:11:51.625 --> 00:11:53.045
So this is a covalent bond.

00:11:53.775 --> 00:11:59.484
This represents that two electrons
are being shared amongst that bond.

00:11:59.964 --> 00:12:03.305
So now what species participate
in this is generally anything

00:12:03.305 --> 00:12:05.135
referred to as molecular.

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:10.719
So, or like a molecule, um, also
binary acids and oxyacids as

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:13.089
well fall under covalent as well.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:17.290
But the main one here is anything
that's molecular, is what you are

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:19.430
looking for, for labeling these.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:23.090
We refer to covalent generally
as molecules, whereas ionic

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:23.789
is generally molecules.

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:28.640
You're cut your compounds being
at these are the hydrogen oxygen

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.030
are both nonmetals, right?

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:30.910
They're the same type.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:36.640
Let's look at the last one So metallic
bonds, which generally does not come up

00:12:36.679 --> 00:12:39.590
a lot in like introductory chemistry or?

00:12:39.985 --> 00:12:44.375
Sometimes it comes up maybe by general
chemistry too, you'll see metallic

00:12:44.375 --> 00:12:49.105
bonds when you're learning about like
maybe coordinate chemistry, um, but it's

00:12:49.105 --> 00:12:52.484
something that doesn't get talked about a
lot unless you're taking a lot of physics

00:12:52.485 --> 00:12:57.474
or engineering, maybe some very heavy
duty bonding courses or in if you're

00:12:57.505 --> 00:12:59.074
trying to learn about semiconductors.

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:04.620
So, what it is, is atoms that are
connected via a sea of electrons,

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.930
meaning that this is between metals only.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.350
So what do I mean by
that sea of electrons?

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:13.860
Well, I have a, let's say I
have this, um, this water bucket

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.220
here and it's made of iron.

00:13:15.579 --> 00:13:19.809
Well, all the iron that's in this,
each one, all the different atoms that

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:22.200
are in it are giving off electrons.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.300
They willingly are giving them away.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:29.339
So because of that, it's causing
all of them to have a very, very

00:13:29.339 --> 00:13:32.120
strong bond amongst themselves.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.190
So this is just more of a demonstration
to see kind of what this is like,

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:40.829
where there's all kinds of wild physics
phenomenon that can happen because of this

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:45.630
sea of electrons, um, with different types
of, different unit, quantum type units.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:50.189
But just keep in mind metallic bonds occur
between metals, and that's all we need

00:13:50.199 --> 00:13:52.939
to know for this, uh, for this video.

